(Firstly: In no way, shape or form is this related to the R. L .Stine
book "Monster Blood." Now, as I was saying...)

Slash'EM Vampire gave me an idea: if we can get potions of ordinary
blood and vampire blood, how about getting potions of a _specific
monster's_ blood?

Similar to how the Slash'EM vampire siphons blood from fresh corpses,
applying a certain item (a syringe, perhaps?) to a fresh, undrained,
blood-filled corpse produces a potion of that monster's blood. Quaffing
the blood has different effects per monster:

Cockatrice blood: toss the potion for the expected petrification, or
quaff it while in golem form.

Nurse blood: for non-humans, this is synonymous with full healing.

Medusa blood: Now this is interesting. It says at
http://www.pantheon.org/articles/a/asclepius.html that blood taken from
the left side of the Gorgon can bring the dead back to life, but blood
taken from the right side kills the person irrevocably. So, quaffing
this one has a 50/50 chance of either killing you, or fully healing you
and (wait for it) conferring **intrinsic lifesaving** for the next 20
turns, or until you make use of it.

King blood: a blue-coloured potion? Okay, that's just a bad joke.

Other monsters: perhaps a lesser chance of providing the intrinsics
that the corpse would have given? (Hmm... in that case, post-drained
corpses shouldn't be able to give intrinsics.)

To prevent unlimited potion abuse, blood potions cannot be diluted
("blood is thicker than water" they say) or cancelled. Polymorphing it
simply changes it into another creature's blood.

